# Plan — etude-9uf.3: Delete the retroactive capture path

## What is being deleted and why

The after-the-fact reconstruction the supervisor model replaces:

| File | Lines | What it did |
|---|---|---|
| `scripts/dogfood-capture.sh` | 139 | Rebuilt five stage artifacts from the bead `design` field and `git show <sha>` AFTER the bead closed. |
| `scripts/dogfood-close.sh` | 146 | Orchestrated the close sequence: preflight, capture run, capture gates, terminal audit. |
| `scripts/dogfood-close_test.sh` | 689 | Fixture tests. **Only partly deletable — see below.** |

Under the worker-lane model every phase artifact exists at gate time and is
captured by `etude capture` as the phase completes, so there is nothing left to
reconstruct. Keeping them would leave two contradictory descriptions of how a
bead is recorded — the failure this epic exists to end.

`scripts/dogfood-gate-capture.sh` is NOT in the deletion set. It remains the
bootstrap path for a hand-assembled gate record, which the supervisor runbook
documents and which this very bead's gate is being recorded through.

### The test file is three suites, and only one of them dies

`scripts/dogfood-close_test.sh` is one file containing three independent
sections. The bead's line count (689) treats them as one deletable unit. They
are not:

| Section | Lines | Cases | Subject | Fate |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A | 269–428 | 11 | `dogfood-close.sh`: happy path, gateless run, unpushed ref, allowlist bypass, capture/gate/audit sequencing, preflight guard. | **Delete** — its subject is deleted. |
| B | 429–642 | 16 | `.beads/hooks/pre-push` ref classification, B1–B10: etude-only exempt, code push audited, gap rejected, mixed refs, deletion-only, tags, empty stdin, unknown refs, missing audit script, and audit-exit-2 fails closed. | **KEEP** |
| C | 643–689 | 2 | `.beads/hooks/pre-push` bd/stdin coexistence, C1: the bd block does not consume stdin, so the dogfood classifier still sees the buffered refs. | **KEEP** |

Sections B and C do not test the close script at all. Both drive
`$PRE_PUSH_HOOK` directly with synthetic stdin and an injected
`DOGFOOD_HOOK_AUDIT_SCRIPT` sentinel. Their subject is the hook this bead is
*editing* and which stays live afterwards.

Deleting all 689 lines would drop the only coverage of a push gate that fires on
every push in this checkout, including its fail-closed branch. The acceptance's
"the three scripts are gone" does not authorize that: the bead scopes the
deletion by what the retroactive path *is*, and B and C are not part of it.

**So: extract Sections B and C to `scripts/pre-push_test.sh`** and delete the
rest of the file. Net deletion from this file is ~428 lines, not 689 — the
bead's line count is corrected rather than met by dropping tests.

**What Section C is and is not.** An earlier draft of this plan claimed C1 pins a
property no B case covers, and that a hook change breaking bd/stdin coexistence
"would leave every B case green". That is false, and the correction matters
because a negative control was resting on it. C1 (lines 653–676) is an inline
duplicate of B2's flow: the same synthetic `refs/heads/main …` line, the same
`audit_sentinel`, the same two assertions, differing only in not calling
`run_hook_split`. B2 fails identically under that breakage. C1 is still worth
keeping — it is real coverage, it is named in the bead's subject area, and
deleting it silently is still the wrong way to lose it — but it is redundant
coverage, not unique coverage, and the plan should not have claimed otherwise.

### What the extraction must carry

The surviving suites depend on setup defined up in the Section A region. Copying
only the cases would produce a suite that runs but proves less. The extraction
carries, and the implementation must verify each is present:

| Lines | What | Why B/C need it |
|---|---|---|
| 27 | `PRE_PUSH_HOOK="$REPO_ROOT/.beads/hooks/pre-push"` | the system under test. |
| 43–45 | `pass_count`, `fail_count`, **`current_test=""`** | the counters drive the exit code; `current_test` is set by `t_start` and read by `t_pass`/`t_fail`. |
| 47–86 | `t_start` / `t_pass` / `t_fail` / `assert_exit` | every case calls these. |
| 88–104 | throwaway repo + bare origin fixture (`tmpdir`, `bare_origin`, `work_repo`), initial commit, the `EXIT` trap | cases `cd "$work_repo"` and push to `$bare_origin`. |
| **106–121** | **the bd PATH shim (`BD_SHIM` + `export PATH="$tmpdir:$PATH"`)** | **load-bearing and easy to miss.** The hook runs its bd block before the classifier. Without the shim the suite runs against whatever real `bd` is on the machine, and cases can be masked by its exit status. |

Two adjustments the copy must make, not just carry:

- **The `EXIT` trap must be trimmed to `rm -rf "$tmpdir"`.** Line 89 is
  `trap 'rm -rf "$tmpdir" "$PREBUILT_ETUDE_DIR"' EXIT`, and `PREBUILT_ETUDE_DIR`
  belongs to the prebuild block that is deliberately not carried. Under
  `set -u` the trap would error on every exit.
- Explicitly NOT carried: the top-of-file `go build ./cmd/etude` and the
  `DOGFOOD_AUDIT_ETUDE_BIN` export. B and C inject sentinels and never invoke
  the real audit or the etude binary.

