## Verification

- **The widened sweep check.** `git ls-files -z | xargs -0 grep -nE
  'dogfood[-_ ](capture|close)'` over tracked files returns **exactly the
  enumerated no-op set above, and nothing else.** Anchoring on `.sh` is what let
  r2's check pass over a live wrong pointer, so the check now matches the
  mechanism names in every form they appear — hyphenated, underscored, spaced,
  with or without extension.
- `make test` and `make lint` green.
- `make docs-check` and `make docs-reality` green.
- **`make pre-push-test` passes all 18 cases — B1–B10 AND C1.** Case-count parity
  is the check: the pre-extraction file reports 29 passed (11 in A, 18 in B+C),
  so the extracted suite must report exactly 18. A suite reporting 16 has
  silently dropped C1.
- `bash examples/summarize/walkthrough.sh` still runs end to end.
- `make dogfood-audit` still runs (it is .4's to replace, not mine to break).
- `make -n dogfood-close-test` fails with "No rule to make target".

### Negative controls

1. **Hook wiring** — temporarily break the `refs/etude/*` exemption branch in the
   hook so it falls through to the audit, and confirm `make pre-push-test`
   FAILS. Proves `$PRE_PUSH_HOOK` resolves to the real, live hook rather than a
   stale path.
2. **bd shim carriage** — the shim's presence is verified **by inspection** of
   the extracted file (the `BD_SHIM` heredoc and the `export PATH` line must both
   be there), plus a run with the `export PATH` line removed to confirm suite
   behavior changes at all. An earlier draft claimed this control would make
   "C1 specifically" fail; that is not observable, because C1 duplicates B2's
   flow and shares the dependency, and on a machine with a real `bd` the hook may
   exit 3 and leave both green. Inspection is the actual guarantee here; the run
   is a weaker corroboration, and this plan no longer claims more for it.

Both controls are reverted immediately after observing the result.

## Risks

- **The pre-push hook is live, and it is on the path of every push.** A syntax
  error or a bad edit there does not produce a wrong message, it blocks pushes
  repo-wide for every lane sharing this checkout — a blast radius larger than
  this bead. Mitigation: the edit is confined to two message/comment strings and
  changes no control flow; verify with `bash -n`, confirm the diff touches no
  `if`/`exit`/test construct, and exercise it by pushing after the change.
- **The deletion could have taken live coverage with it.** Two thirds of the
  named test file tests the deleted script; one third tests a hook that
  survives. A line-count-driven deletion would have passed `make test` and
  `make lint` cleanly while removing the only tests of a live push gate —
  because `make test` is `go test ./...` and has never invoked any shell suite.
- **Nothing runs the shell suites automatically, and the `shell-test` aggregate
  does not change that.** There is no CI in this repo (`.github/workflows/` does
  not exist), so `make test` staying green proves nothing about any shell suite.
  Both seats asked for the aggregate and it is worth adding — one command now
  runs every shell suite, and a new suite has an obvious place to be listed —
  but it is discoverability, not enforcement. Nothing invokes it unless a person
  does. Claiming otherwise would be exactly the kind of green-check-over-nothing
  this bead's own gate rounds kept catching. Closing the gap for real means
  either CI or a pre-push hook that runs it, and neither is in scope here.
- **Deleting ~713 lines removes the only executable description of the old
  protocol.** Git history keeps it, and bead .2 already removed the prose that
  described it; leaving the scripts would leave the repo able to *perform* a
  protocol its own docs no longer describe.

## Corrections to this plan

Recorded rather than silently amended, because a plan asserting a state the repo
is not actually in is the exact failure this bead exists to remove.

1. **Sweep table omitted `scripts/dogfood-gate-capture.sh`**, and the
   verification asserted a grep result that draft would not have produced.
2. **The Risks section claimed the pre-push hook was not installed.** It is:
   `core.hooksPath` points at `.beads/hooks`, so the tracked file IS the running
   hook.
3. **The deletion set treated `dogfood-close_test.sh` as a single unit** because
   the bead does. It is three suites, two of which test a hook that survives.
4. **Round 1, both seats BLOCK.** codex: the completeness-audit exemption
   violates the acceptance outright, and the `.workmux` exemption needed proof.
   opus: three sections not two, and the dependency list omitted the bd PATH
   shim.
5. **Round 2, codex GO / opus BLOCK.** opus found a missed sweep site the plan's
   own verification structurally could not catch — `review-gate-runbook.md:346`
   names `dogfood-close` without an extension, and the check was anchored on
   `.sh`. It also showed the Section C rationale was overstated and that the
   second negative control rested on that overstatement.

**The theme, and why this round is different.** Three of these five are one
failure: a verification claim that does not cover what it says it covers. Per
the supervisor runbook's own rule — when a seat names a new site for a finding
you already fixed, stop patching the named site and enumerate every place the
invariant must hold — this round replaces the narrow grep with an exhaustive
tracked-file inventory of all 25 hits in 13 files, classified into 10 edit sites
and an enumerated no-op baseline, and it corrects the two claims that were
overstated rather than defending them. That audit also surfaced the
`review-gate-runbook.md` section being stale on two further counts beyond the
one the seat reported.
